1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,998
The brush uncovers palm leaves covered in small, careful Tamil script.

2
00:00:04,998 --> 00:00:12,445
Palm leaf does not last in a tropical climate — a manuscript survives only because somebody copied it before the old one rotted,

3
00:00:12,445 --> 00:00:14,419
generation after generation.

4
00:00:16,767 --> 00:00:21,639
Everything in this episode reached us because people kept doing that for two thousand years.

5
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:26,279
Almost entirely from the texts themselves,

6
00:00:26,279 --> 00:00:31,734
which were edited and printed in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries from surviving manuscripts.

7
00:00:31,734 --> 00:00:38,089
Early Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions give some corroboration — names, places, and the fact that writing was in use.

8
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:46,483
Tradition says there were three Sangams, academies of poets at Madurai, under royal patronage.

9
00:00:46,483 --> 00:00:51,652
The first two are described as impossibly ancient and their works are said to be lost.

10
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:59,263
The third is the one that produced the poetry we actually have.

11
00:00:59,263 --> 00:01:05,961
Historians treat the story of the three academies as a later tradition rather than a record — but the poetry itself is real,

12
00:01:05,961 --> 00:01:08,297
and that is what the exam asks about.

13
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:16,093
The corpus is organised and the organisation is examinable.

14
00:01:16,093 --> 00:01:18,539
Eight anthologies, the Ettuthokai.

15
00:01:18,539 --> 00:01:20,845
Ten long poems, the Pattuppattu.

16
00:01:20,845 --> 00:01:22,962
And eighteen minor works.

17
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:31,525
Standing apart and earlier is the Tolkappiyam, the oldest surviving Tamil grammar — and far more than a grammar,

18
00:01:31,525 --> 00:01:37,446
because it also sets out the conventions of poetry and the rules of the five landscapes we met last time.

19
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:44,028
Here is the division that organises all of it.

20
00:01:44,028 --> 00:01:47,476
Akam means the inside: poems of love and private feeling,

21
00:01:47,476 --> 00:01:53,067
in which the people are deliberately left unnamed, so the situation stands for anyone's.

22
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,037
Puram means the outside: poems of war, kings, death, generosity and public life, in which people are named, praised and mourned.

23
00:02:04,037 --> 00:02:11,689
Akam and puram together are how Tamil poetics divides human experience, and the pairing is a favourite exam question.

24
00:02:18,367 --> 00:02:21,228
What does the poetry tell us about the society?

25
00:02:21,228 --> 00:02:27,605
That it had kings and many smaller chieftains; that patronage of poets was a public duty and a reputation;

26
00:02:27,605 --> 00:02:32,852
that trade and towns mattered; that women composed, and are among the named poets.

27
00:02:35,867 --> 00:02:43,607
It also shows distinctions of occupation and status, though the later, rigid fourfold varna order is not what these poems describe.

28
00:02:43,607 --> 00:02:50,592
Read them as evidence, but remember they are poems: they praise, they idealise, and they are written for patrons.

29
00:02:57,067 --> 00:02:59,782
So how much of the Sangam story is history?

30
00:02:59,782 --> 00:03:07,672
One view holds that it preserves a genuine memory of organised, royally patronised literary activity in the Tamil country.

31
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:16,668
The other holds that the three-academy scheme is a much later framing, built to give the poetry an ancient pedigree,

32
00:03:16,668 --> 00:03:22,097
and that only the surviving poems can be dated — roughly to the first few centuries of the common era.

33
00:03:22,097 --> 00:03:25,138
Give both, and keep the poems and the legend apart.

34
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:34,305
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:34,305 --> 00:03:37,177
Akam is the inside — love, unnamed.

36
00:03:37,177 --> 00:03:41,068
Puram is the outside — war and kings, named.

37
00:03:45,733 --> 00:03:46,455
Dig Quiz.

38
00:03:46,455 --> 00:03:47,827
Pause and name it.

39
00:03:47,827 --> 00:03:54,396
First: the oldest surviving Tamil grammar, which also lays down the conventions of poetry.

40
00:03:54,396 --> 00:03:55,923
The Tolkappiyam.

41
00:03:58,767 --> 00:04:02,122
Second: the eight anthologies of the Sangam corpus.

42
00:04:02,122 --> 00:04:03,154
The Ettuthokai.

43
00:04:03,154 --> 00:04:09,842
Third: the category of poems about love and private feeling, in which the people are left unnamed.

44
00:04:12,667 --> 00:04:13,056
Akam.

45
00:04:13,056 --> 00:04:19,124
In Mains, a common question asks you to assess Sangam literature as a historical source.

46
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:27,730
Say what it is good for — society, trade, patronage, landscape — and be honest about its limits:

47
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:32,514
it is poetry, written for patrons, and difficult to date precisely.

48
00:04:37,167 --> 00:04:43,154
A palm-leaf manuscript takes its place in the gallery, saved by copying rather than by lasting.

49
00:04:45,433 --> 00:04:51,249
Next gallery: the ships, the ports and the gold — how far ancient India actually traded.

50
00:04:51,249 --> 00:04:55,273
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

